  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/6/64/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_22.png",
  "plot_summary": "The story opens with Reed running tests on Sue's invisibility power.&nbsp; Predictably, Johnny and Ben get into a tussle that results in the Thing trying to douse Johnny with a canister of chemical foam.&nbsp; Sue is in the direct line of fire, and suddenly, an invisible barrier blocks the foam!&nbsp; Reed is thrilled that she has \"created a shield of invisible energy!\"&nbsp; For the first time, Sue realizes that her invisibility power is a form of energy that she can fashion into a protective screen!\n\nAs Reed encourages her, she fashions a more powerful invisible screen that both Ben and Johnny attempt to penetrate, to no avail.&nbsp; Reed thinks this is only the beginning!&nbsp; Later in the issue, Sue discovers she can make other things invisible too -- though not at the same time she herself is invisible -- she can direct her field of invisibility at only one thing at a time.\n\nA NYPD police officer suddenly enters, who's obviously intended to be none other than Francis Muldoon (Fred Gwynne) from the TV show <i>Car 54 Where Are You?</i>  He's here to report a complaint about the ICBM they keep on the premises, which he thinks violates zoning laws.&nbsp; Suddenly the nearby phones ring, and when Ben answers it, he's beset with complaints about this and that -- noise, sudden shaking of the building, scaring cats, weapons on hand, etc.&nbsp; The FF are making the building undesirable for the other tenants!&nbsp; And a sculptor shows up as well to complain about his sculpture getting melted when the Human Torch flew past his window!&nbsp; The Women's Canasta society shows up to ask them to stop their \"dangerous experiments.\"&nbsp; Reed even fields a call from a \"Mrs. Frobush\" where he says \"I don't know how you got our number, but <i>honest</i>...this <i>isn't</i> Sam's Delicatessen!!\"\n\nAfter Sue helps get rid of the intruders, Reed realizes that Sue never need worry again about feeling as if she didn't contribute enough to the team!&nbsp; He even goes so far to suggest that she might actually be the \"star member!\"&nbsp; But with all the complaints about what they do constantly in the building, Reed also realizes that they do indeed need to find a more secluded spot for some of their larger experiments.&nbsp; He sees a pamphlet about a \"small deserted island for sale off the coast of New Jersey,\" and they take off to go have a look at it.\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\nAs they approach, they see <i>why</i> it's deserted - surrounded by \"an impenetrable barrier reef.\"&nbsp; It's an eerie place to be sure, and seemingly on cue, their \"u-car\" is pulled underwater, and a figure appears in the shadows.&nbsp; He rushes into a cave as the four pursue.&nbsp; Lo and behold, it's their first enemy, the Mole Man!&nbsp; He welcomes them to his \"kingdom of Subterranea!\"&nbsp; He lured them here with all those complaints from the building tenants, and now they fall into this trap, a \"cage\" where the walls are radioactive!&nbsp; (As they fall, Ben laments that his \"dear ol' Aunt Petunia\" warned him about the company he kept - her first <i>ever</i> mention! - see note below.)\n\nWhen Ben heads over to the wall to climb it, Reed pulls him back, calling him both a \"muscle-bound numbskull\" and a \"lumbering deadhead!\" (<i>Wow, pretty crude words for Reed!</i>)&nbsp; Reed is obviously upset at himself for leading them into this.&nbsp; Johnny even thinks he's never seen Reed so grim.\n\nThe Mole Man then explains how he escaped underground when Monster Isle was destroyed (back in [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1|2026-03-22]).&nbsp; And now he's planning to cause major cities to sink below the Earth, making the US and Soviet Union think the other was responsible, leading to a surface war!&nbsp; But as he reaches for the control device, he discovers that he cannot touch it!&nbsp; Sue has projected an invisible force bubble around it!&nbsp; Reed suggests she surround them instead so that they can approach the wall, shielded by Sue's invisible energy.&nbsp; (<i>I don't know why Johnny didn't just fly directly out of there - go figure</i>!)\n\nNow outside of the cage, they attack!&nbsp; But each is drawn into a room designed to hold them, and the next couple of pages show each one using their own ingenuity to escape.&nbsp; But even as they emerge to face him again, he plunges the room into darkness and uses his \"radar sense\" to evade them (sure, enough, as advertised in the editorial blurb, this was indeed mentioned in [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1|2026-03-22]).&nbsp; Reed and Ben fumble about in the darkness for a bit before the Torch sheds some light on things.\n\nReed orders them back to the now-discovered \"u-car,\" seeming to be really stressed about it, telling them they need to go <i>now!</i>  The Mole Man makes his way back to his control device, intending to sink the cities as advertised, but he learns (literally on the very last panel) that Reed has \"rearranged the main circuit wiring so that nothing sinks beneath the Earth's surface except the Mole Man's isle <i>itself</i>, destroying all of his complex scientific equipment in one man inundating tide of water...\" as the FF escape.\n\nYes, all that was in the <i>very last panel</i>!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "A <i>very</i> difficult one to grade.\n\n[ASSET: left | InvisibleForceField | width=12%] [SPAN: severity=success; text=On the positive side], we have the Invisible Girl <i>finally</i> being given powers enough to actually make a significant contribution to the group.&nbsp; Up until this point, Lee and Kirby seemed to have to go out of their way to find something she could do.&nbsp; That will no longer be necessary, as she has been given a nice set of abilities.\n\nThey did a great job introducing them and showing off how she can use them.\n[ASSET: center | SuesNewPowers | width=67%]\n\nBut there were [SPAN: severity=success; text=<i>more </i>positives] to be found here, namely:\n[HANG] * the playful way that Ben and Johnny were bickering (I especially loved panel 4 on page 2)\n[HANG] * the page 4 cameo appearance by NYPD patrolman Francis Muldoon.&nbsp; Though not named, no one familiar with television at the time could mistake actor Fred Gwynne's face from the TV show <i>Car 54, Where Are You?</i> (which had been canceled by the time this issue came out, but that fact was perhaps as yet unknown to Lee and Kirby at the time of creation).&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Fred Gwynne would later become known for playing Herman Munster on the 1964-1966 show, <i>The Munsters</i>.]\n[HANG] * the complaints about the ICBM the FF keeps on the premises, which \"violates the <i>zoning </i>law for this neighborhood.\"\n[HANG] * the other absurd complaints that building tenants have about the FF occupying the upper floors of the Baxter Building, which Ben attributes to \"professional <i>gripers</i>.\"\n[HANG] * the Women's Canasta society shows up to ask them to stop their \"dangerous experiments,\" fearful that if anything goes wrong, \"you're liable to make this whole neighborhood radioactive!\" (and right in the middle of their <i>tournament</i> - [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Horrors!])\n\n[ASSET: right | SamsDelicatessen | width=12%] But the highlight of the absurdity comes on page 7, when Reed answers the phone and has to tell \"Mrs. Frobush\" that she has the wrong number and that <i>honestly, </i>\"This <i>isn't</i> Sam's Delicatessen!!\"&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: To my knowledge, this is the first time \"Frobush\" is mentioned in a comic, later morphing to the more familiar \"Forbush\" (namely Irving).]\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=Reed's absolutely exacerbated face is simply <i>classic</i> and made me laugh out loud.]\n[BR]\n[BR] The issue later indicates that the various complains were orchestrated by the Mole Man himself, but you <i>do</i> still have to wonder why things like this hadn't actually come up before.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text= I suspect he didn't really have to twist anyone's arms too much.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nOf course, the many complaints is only setup to drive Reed to find a better place to \"conduct our larger experiments,\" leading directly to him looking at a pamphlet they got \"in the mail <i>today</i>!\" [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(How convenient!)]&nbsp; It advertises one of the most unlikely places of all time:&nbsp; \"a small, deserted island for sale, off the coast of New Jersey!\"\n\n[ASSET: left | BuyingAnIsland | width=23%] <b><i>Cheap</i></b>, no less, and they <i>immediately</i> race out to buy it!\n[BR]\n[BR] The unlikelihood of there being a \"deserted\" island off the New Jersey coast aside, you'll note that, once <i>again</i>, Jack Kirby forgets that the Baxter Building has <i>five</i> upper floors, not <i>four</i> as shown in this panel.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And you gotta <i>love</i> the greenery up on top!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=How come we never <i>see</i> this on roof shots?)]\n\n\n<b>FIXME - continue here</b>\n\n\nOn the negative side, the Mole Man?&nbsp; <i>Really</i>?&nbsp; I know it was advertised as being the FF vs their very first foe, but he has never been a particularly memorable one, and his plan to sink the cities and start WW3?&nbsp; Seems like a lot of work!&nbsp; And the fact that no one noticed that the city was being set up on a huge platform to sink in the first place?&nbsp; I'm not buying it.\n\nThe death traps were just that:&nbsp; death traps, which are visually clever, but nevertheless formulaic, particularly when it comes to the FF.&nbsp; You can almost count on it being either a page or a half-page of each one figuring out how to elude them.\n\nAnd as for the ending -- \"<i>Holy lack of space, Batman</i>!\"&nbsp; Wow, that was about as abrupt an ending as I've ever seen before, requiring the last panel to have so much text added to it just to explain what happened!&nbsp; It's as if Kirby lost track of things when he was busy showing them getting out of their various death traps and suddenly realized \"Dang, this is the last page!\"&nbsp; I've never seen so much crammed into one page to wrap things up.&nbsp; Poor planning, which affected the final grade.&nbsp; (And I guess New Jersey is out one \"deserted island\" off the coast to boot!)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe mixed quality of the issue relegates it to mere mid-grade status.&nbsp; Not a <i>bad</i> issue, and it certainly had its moments, but hardly a great one either!\n\n[ASSET: left | Absurdities | width=27%] There's no denying that the first third of the book seems to intentionally showcase a whole <i>host</i> of absurdities.\n[BR]\n[BR] Jack Kirby appeared to be having a lot of fun leaning into it, both with the <i>wink, wink</i> cameo by Fred Gwynne, and <i>especially</i> with the different facial expressions, topped by Reed's phone conversation with Mrs. Frobush, not to mention the terror on the faces of the ladies of the Canasta club as they scattered at the top of page 8.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=The comedy was top-notch], and it went far beyond the mundane bickering readers <i>normally</i> find dominating the opening pages of this title on a monthly basis.\n\nBut let's not overlook the fact that these pages <i>also </i>do the important work of introducing [SPAN: severity=info; text=Sue's newly-discovered invisibility powers], particularly her ability to cast force fields.&nbsp; The fact that this was so seamlessly weaved amidst the various neighbors' largely ridiculous complaints about the hazards of living in the same building as the Fantastic Four only highlights the brilliance of both co-creators, and at the same time almost seamlessly gives Invisible Girl a <i>major</i> overhaul -- all of which benefits the long-term outlook of the magazine.\n\nBut of course... [SPAN: severity=error; text=it was followed by the villain-of-the-month], which the cover so blatantly advertises as \"the first super-villain the FF ever faced!\" [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(like that's somehow a <i>good</i> thing...)]\n\n\n<b>FIXME</b> - the following statement is no longer true...........................\n\nFinally, note that the Marvel database posts that the first mention of Aunt Petunia is <i>Fantastic Four</i> #25, which is clearly incorrect since she is mentioned in this issue.&nbsp; (I learned this when I was researching to find out if this was indeed her first mention.&nbsp; It is -- you can see the text for yourself!)\n\nThis issue is also notable for being the first time Ben hollers out his famous \"It's <i>clobberin' time</i>!\" war-cry (on page 15).",
  "letters_page": "This issue features a letter from \"Dave Cockrum\" (at that point apparently a college student in Carbondale, IL), who of course would go on to become a well-known artist and later writer, and a co-creator of the New X-Men.\n\nAnother letter from a guy named \"Roy Thomas,\" who complains that he went to the store and ended up spending a whole $1.95 on comic books -- all of them Marvel!&nbsp; And he points out that he buys two copies of each one!&nbsp; (I hope he held onto them!)\n\nAnd I liked the comment Stephen Erickson made about the <i>Incredible Hulk</i> -- that the first two issues were great \"and ended up a waste of paper.\"&nbsp; (<i>Haha</i>, so true!&nbsp; But I wouldn't have even granted them two issues.)\n\nThe Announcements section explains why they use a limited number of artists (essentially consisting of Jack Kirby, Steve Ditko and Don Heck at this early stage).",
  "ownership": "My own VG/FN or so copy of this book came as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $20.&nbsp; It ultimately sold on eBay for $139 in Dec 2024, netting me over $98.",
  "highlight": "FIXME -- write something"
}